  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0151 database is designed to manage various aspects of an organization's operations, including user management, asset tracking, and maintenance scheduling. The database contains 14 tables, each representing a distinct entity. The announcements table stores messages from users, with each announcement linked to the user who created it. The assets_departments table establishes relationships between assets and departments, with each asset belonging to one department and each department potentially containing multiple assets. The password_resets table stores tokens for resetting user passwords, with each token linked to a unique email address. The categories table organizes assets into hierarchical categories, with each category potentially having a parent category. The invoices table tracks financial transactions, with each invoice linked to a user and containing details such as amount, due date, and status. The assets table stores information about physical assets, including name, serial number, manufacturer, category, location, department, and user, with each asset linked to its manufacturer, category, location, department, and user. The api_tokens table manages API access tokens for users, with each token linked to a user and containing details such as name, token, and expiration date. The notifications table stores messages sent to users, with each notification linked to a user and containing a message and a read status. The tickets table tracks issues related to assets, with each ticket linked to an asset and a user, and containing details such as status and description. The maintenance table schedules maintenance activities for assets, with each maintenance activity linked to an asset and containing details such as description and date. The departments table stores information about organizational departments, with each department linked to a location. The locations table stores information about physical locations, with each location containing details such as name and address. The manufacturers table stores information about manufacturers of assets, with each manufacturer containing details such as name. Finally, the users table stores information about users, with each user containing details such as email, name, password, and remember token.",
  "gold_question": "Background / Context\n\nThe project involves developing an open-source IT asset management system called Snipe-IT. This system is designed to assist organizations in efficiently tracking and visualizing various assets including hardware, software licenses, accessories, and consumables. Built on the Laravel framework, Snipe-IT provides a user-friendly web interface that facilitates asset management tasks.\n\nObjectives\n\nThe primary objectives of Snipe-IT are to enable organizations to:\n- Track and manage IT assets effectively.\n- Visualize asset histories and statuses.\n- Implement efficient asset check-in and check-out processes.\n- Generate and utilize barcodes for asset tracking.\n- Send automated email notifications for asset-related updates.\n- Manage user access through role-based controls.\n- Integrate with LDAP/Active Directory for streamlined user management.\n- Utilize an API for automation and enhanced integration.\n\nStakeholders & Users\n\nThe main stakeholders and users of Snipe-IT include:\n- IT teams in businesses, schools, and non-profits.\n- System administrators responsible for asset tracking and management.\n- End-users who interact with and check out assets.\n- Organizational decision-makers interested in analytics and reporting.\n\nFunctional Descriptions\n\n1. Asset Management: \n   - Track hardware, software licenses, accessories, and consumables.\n   - Maintain detailed asset histories including maintenance and ticket records.\n   - Perform asset check-in and check-out operations.\n\n2. Barcode Generation: \n   - Generate barcodes for assets to facilitate physical tracking.\n   \n3. Notifications: \n   - Send automated email alerts for asset updates, upcoming maintenance, and more.\n\n4. Role-Based Access Control: \n   - Implement permissions and access controls based on user roles.\n   \n5. LDAP/Active Directory Integration: \n   - Align user management with organizational directories.\n   \n6. API Access: \n   - Provide API endpoints for external integration and automation.\n\n7. Multi-User and Multi-Location Support: \n   - Enable system use across various user profiles and geographical locations.\n\nNon-Functional Descriptions\n\n1. Usability: \n   - Ensure a user-friendly web interface that is easy to navigate.\n\n2. Scalability: \n   - Support multiple users and locations without degradation of performance.\n\n3. Flexibility: \n   - Allow for customization and self-hosting to meet diverse organizational needs.\n\n4. Security: \n   - Ensure secure data handling, access control, and integration processes.\n\n5. Reliability: \n   - Maintain consistent performance and availability across installations.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_llama31/0151.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0151\nCREATE TABLE `users` (\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `remember_token` varchar(100) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`user_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `manufacturers` (\n  `manufacturer_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`manufacturer_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `locations` (\n  `location_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `address` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`location_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `departments` (\n  `department_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `location_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`department_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `departments_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`location_id`) REFERENCES `locations` (`location_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `categories` (\n  `category_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `parent_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`category_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `categories_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`parent_id`) REFERENCES `categories` (`category_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `assets` (\n  `asset_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `serial_number` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `manufacturer_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `category_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `location_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `department_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`asset_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `assets_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`manufacturer_id`) REFERENCES `manufacturers` (`manufacturer_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `assets_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`category_id`) REFERENCES `categories` (`category_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `assets_ibfk_3` FOREIGN KEY (`location_id`) REFERENCES `locations` (`location_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `assets_ibfk_4` FOREIGN KEY (`department_id`) REFERENCES `departments` (`department_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `assets_ibfk_5` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`user_id`) ON DELETE SET NULL ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `invoices` (\n  `invoice_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `amount` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `due_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`invoice_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `invoices_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `api_tokens` (\n  `token_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `token` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `expires_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`token_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `api_tokens_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `announcements` (\n  `announcement_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `message` text NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`announcement_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `announcements_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `notifications` (\n  `notification_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `message` text NOT NULL,\n  `read_status` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`notification_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `notifications_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `password_resets` (\n  `reset_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `token` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`reset_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `tickets` (\n  `ticket_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `asset_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`ticket_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `tickets_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`asset_id`) REFERENCES `assets` (`asset_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `tickets_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `maintenance` (\n  `maintenance_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `asset_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text NOT NULL,\n  `maintenance_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`maintenance_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `maintenance_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`asset_id`) REFERENCES `assets` (`asset_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
